ng the existing project structure and references? Agentic Workflows: If using an automated LLM-assisted script (e.g., using an agentic framework designed for refactoring), how can we best supply the complex Java dependency graph to the parser to ensure the resulting TypeScript types and imports align correctly? Any insights into systematic, scalable approaches for this type of cross-language migration would be highly appreciated. Stack Overflow query: manual workflow automation https://stackoverflow.com/questions/[phone]/strategies-for-bulk-migrating-a-large-java-jar-codebase-and-its-proprietary-depe', 'Strategies for bulk-migrating a large Java/JAR codebase and its proprietary dependencies to Node.js/TypeScript Strategi…', '[\"to\", \"java\", \"we\", \"an\", \"dependencies\", \"typescript\", \"codebase\", \"proprietary\"]', 'neutral', 4, 0, NULL, 0, 1, 1.00, 'v1');
INSERT INTO `cleaned_signal` VALUES ('clean_27fa701653e547919e1792880034c0f6', 'signal_77be48ae83e34fa4a56c53f4c0929905', 'Is maintaining manual per-file and per-function change logs alongside Git considered a professional practice in 2026? Is maintaining manual per-file and per-function change logs alongside Git considered a professional practice in 2026? Is maintaining manual per-file and per-function change logs alongside Git considered a professional practice in 2026? I’m working on a project where we already use modern version control practices: Git with GitFlow branching Pull requests with mandatory reviews Conventional commit messages CI-based checks Despite this, our internal engineering guideline mandates manual change tracking inside source files , in addition to Git history. I’d like feedback from experienced engineers on whether this approach is considered legitimate and professional in 2026 , or if it is generally viewed as redundant in modern workflows. Requirement 1: Mandatory File-Level Modification History Every source file must contain a large comment block at the top that manually tracks changes, including author, date, version, and description. Example (JavaScript) This is required even though Git already tracks the same information via commits and pull requests. Requirement 2: Mandatory Function-Level Change History In addition to file-level history, each function or method must include a similar modification history inside its DocBlock. Example This is enforced during code review. Requirement 3: Strict Formatting Rules That Override Formatters Always use spaces: Inside parentheses: ( $a, $b ) Inside arrays/objects: [ \'id\' => 1 ], { id: 1 } Around operators: $a + $b, a === b After commas The guideline enforces non-standard spacing rules such as: Instead of formatter defaults: Using a formatter like Prettier is allowed, but developers must manually re-adjust formatting afterward to comply with the guideline. Requirement 4: Mandatory DocBlocks for All Functions Every function — including small, internal helpers — must include a full DocBlock describing: Purpose Parameters Return value Change history Even when the function is trivial or self-explanatory. Concern From a developer productivity and maintainability standpoint, this raises several questions: Git already provides authoritative change history — authorship, timestamps, diffs, reviews Manual logs frequently go out of sync Refactoring requires updating many comment blocks Merge conflicts can occur in comment-only sections Formatting rules conflict with automation tools At the same time, the intent is clearly to improve traceability, readability, and auditability. The Actual Question In modern professional software teams (especially in 2025–2026): Is maintaining manual per-file and per-function change logs alongside Git considered a best practice? Do reputable teams still rely on this pattern, or is Git history generally considered sufficient? Are there specific industries (e.g., fintech, regulated environments) where this is still justified? How do modern teams balance documentation requirements with automation and developer productivity? I’m looking for industry-standard perspectives , not company-specific policies. Stack Overflow query: product feedback workflow https://stackoverflow.com/questions/[phone]/is-maintaining-manual-per-file-and-per-function-change-logs-alongside-git-consid', 'Is maintaining manual per-file and per-function change logs alongside Git considered a professional practice in 2026', '[\"is\", \"git\", \"in\", \"per\", \"change\", \"function\", \"file\", \"history\"]', 'neutral', 3, 0, NULL, 0, 1, 0.90, 'v1');
INSERT INTO `cleaned_signal` VALUES ('clean_282e41e25db04e0aa0a2add8512e72d0', 'signal_54444362ca474c59b169268472e177a1', 'GitHub Actions workflow not scheduling (Schedule trigger not appearing) GitHub Actions workflow not scheduling (Schedule trigger not appearing) GitHub Actions workflow not scheduling (Schedule trigger not appearing) I have developed a Python script and set up a GitHub Actions workflow ( ) to execute it daily at 7 AM KST (22:00 UTC) using a schedule. The issue is that the scheduled automation does not trigger at all. While the (manual trigger) works perfectly fine, there is no indication in the GitHub Actions UI that the workflow is \"scheduled.\" I have already verified the following: No typos in the YAML file. The workflow file is correctly pushed to the default branch ( ). Actions permissions are enabled in the repository settings. The file exists on the default branch. Despite this, the \"clock icon\" or the \"Scheduled\" status is missing from the Actions tab. Has anyone experienced this issue where manual triggers work but the cron schedule is completely ignored by GitHub? I also checked the API ( ) and the state is \'active\', but the \'schedule\' property is missing in the response. Stack Overflow query: manual workflow automation https://stackoverflow.com/questions/[phone]/github-actions-workflow-not-scheduling-schedule-trigger-not-appearing', 'GitHub Actions workflow not scheduling (Schedule trigger not appearing) GitHub Actions workflow not scheduling (Schedul…', '[\"not\", \"actions\", \"is\", \"workflow\", \"github\", \"schedule\", \"trigger\", \"appearing\"]', 'neutral', 2, 0, NULL, 0, 1, 1.00, 'v1');
INSERT INTO `cleaned_signal` VALUES ('clean_29e1440fbb77460baa4283feadeb0f3d', 'signal_f19e8f00a5ff43f7ac56b99b54b2ded8', 'For PowerShell but looking for a cross-platform terminal workflow. For PowerShell but looking for a cross-platform terminal workflow. Extracted demand: For PowerShell but looking for a cross-platform terminal workflow. User pain point: For PowerShell but looking for a cross-platform terminal workflow. Evidence excerpt: About running 2 commands at once, for example to run 2 console programs with 1 single command, in PowerShell, one way to do this is with a command in the next format, just by adding between each of the 2 commands/sub-co… Original source title: How to run 2 commands at once, in parallel, auto-executing each command in a pane. For PowerShell but looking for a cro… Stack Overflow Questions auto discovery query: looking for tool workflow https://stackoverflow.com/questions/[phone]/how-to-run-2-commands-at-once-in-parallel-auto-executing-each-command-in-a-pan', 'For PowerShell but looking for a cross-platform terminal workflow. For PowerShell but looking for a cross-platform term…', '[\"in\", \"looking\", \"powershell\", \"but\", \"workflow\", \"command\", \"commands\", \"cross\"]', 'negative', 2, 0, NULL, 0, 1, 1.00, 'v1');
INSERT INTO `cleaned_signal` VALUES ('clean_2b2eb79d79d242c89d418139f247f517', 'signal_de0f8aca9da1472b98fbc9d91f851cac', 'Mine, an IDE for Coalton and Common Lisp Mine, an IDE for Coalton and Common Lisp Mine, an IDE for Coalton and Common Lisp Yeah, I mean there is some support for various editors ( https:&#x2F;&#x2F;lispcookbook.github.io&#x2F;cl-cookbook&#x2F;editor-support.ht... ) including VS Code ( https:&#x2F;&#x2F;lispcookbook.github.io&#x2F;cl-cookbook&#x2F;vscode-alive.html ), but it&#x27;s kind of rough ( https:&#x2F;&#x2F;blog.djhaskin.com&#x2F;blog&#x2F;experience-report-using-vs-co... ) and not exactly feature-complete with the emacs experience, plus you&#x27;re still left having to figure out how to install and setup a Lisp implementation and quicklisp. I like that mine solves those for a newcomer, especially on Windows. (I myself use vim + slimv, but even that isn&#x27;t quite at parity in some respects with emacs. The biggest weaknesses are around debugging, especially in the presence of multiple threads. But the essentials do work (stepping, eval-in-frame, continuing-from-a-stack-frame, selecting the various types of restarts, compiling changes before selecting restarts) so I&#x27;m still fairly productive and don&#x27;t feel like I&#x27;m lacking anything sorely needed for professional work. I&#x27;ve hacked together some automatic refactoring bits as well, which emacs doesn&#x27;t have either, and I&#x27;m eventually going to make a separated GUI test runner.) I&#x27;ve been kicking the tires with mine a little bit yesterday and today, I think it&#x27;s quite good for the beginner experience. But I&#x27;m constantly of two minds about reporting some feature requests. The project&#x27;s primary goal seems to be existing as a stepping stone to even see what Lisp (and especially Coalton) is really all about before \"graduating\" to something like emacs, it feels like a secondary goal (though it is mentioned as a goal) to be usable by professionals as well, but there&#x27;s inherent tension there. That&#x27;s also been a weakness with the other editors: anyone already comfortable with Lisp development, professional or not, in emacs or not, isn&#x27;t very likely to give the time of day to some new thing that&#x27;s almost certainly not going to be as good as what they&#x27;re used to. And so the new thing doesn&#x27;t get the attention and feedback from experienced developers and the gap never closes. Hacker News comment query: feature request https://coalton-lang.github.io/mine/', 'm lacking anything sorely needed for professional work. I&#x27', '[\"x27\", \"x2f\", \"to\", \"as\", \"lisp\", \"mine\", \"but\", \"coalton\"]', 'positive', 3, 0, NULL, 0, 0, 1.00, 'v1');
INSERT INTO `cleaned_signal` VALUES ('clean_2d976a0cc4a649f4afa5cf6d318be497', 'signal_b6021d2ba170463c9f2f4c414b24c096', 'Using coding assistance tools to revive projects you never were going to finish Using coding assistance tools to revive projects you never were going to finish Using coding assistance tools to revive projects you never were going to finish The tooling landscape has changed so much in the last two years that I find myself re-evaluating automation setups that were solid 18 months ago. The time investment to rebuild is real but the efficiency gains on the other side are worth it for anything you&#x27;re doing more than a few times a week. Hacker News comment query: automation pain https://blog.matthewbrunelle.com/its-ok-to-use-coding-assistance-tools-to-revive-the-projects-you-never-were-going-to-finish/', 're doing more than a few times a week. Hacker News comment query: automation pain https://blog.matthewbrunelle.com/its-…', '[\"to\", \"were\", \"you\", \"assistance\", \"coding\", \"finish\", \"going\", \"never\"]', 'negative', 2, 0, NULL, 0, 0, 1.00, 'v1');
