INSERT INTO `cleaned_signal` VALUES ('clean_77506ef9dfb34a3ca39c206ff8f79669', 'signal_0395bf06d5d64310a1f3e81812d3d5ae', 'How do you fully automate a Kaggle notebook that requires a kernel restart mid-run after installing a library? How do you fully automate a Kaggle notebook that requires a kernel restart mid-run after installing a library? How do you fully automate a Kaggle notebook that requires a kernel restart mid-run after installing a library? looking for advice on a workflow problem rather than a specific library issue. The situation I have a notebook that: Installs packages via pip in Cell 1 Needs a kernel restart for the new packages to load correctly (C extension ABI conflict with pre-installed packages) Otherwise the code gets stuck And here is the error message I am getting, Manual restarting fixed it,but I can\'t save and run. Then runs the actual pipeline in the remaining cells The manual workflow is: run Cell 1 → restart kernel → click Run All. This works fine. But I want to submit this as a fully automated pipeline that runs end to end with zero manual steps — no clicking, no babysitting. What I actually want A pattern where I can hit Run All exactly once and the entire pipeline completes — installs, restarts (if needed), and continues execution automatically. My questions Is there a known pattern for auto-resuming execution after a programmatic kernel restart in Kaggle notebooks? Is the right answer to just restructure the notebook as a plain script and run it via the terminal — and if so, how do you handle output/logging back to the notebook? How do others handle this class of problem — packages that conflict with Kaggle\'s pre-installed environment and need isolation? I appriciate any help — even a \"this is fundamentally impossible, here\'s why\" would be useful at this point! Thanks! Stack Overflow query: manual workflow automation https://stackoverflow.com/questions/[phone]/how-do-you-fully-automate-a-kaggle-notebook-that-requires-a-kernel-restart-mid-r', 'looking for advice on a workflow problem rather than a specific library issue. The situation I have a notebook that: In…', '[\"run\", \"kernel\", \"notebook\", \"restart\", \"do\", \"how\", \"kaggle\", \"fully\"]', 'neutral', 4, 0, NULL, 0, 1, 1.00, 'v1');
