INSERT INTO `cleaned_signal` VALUES ('clean_7f27230898a540bb852f1aa4eeea4e8e', 'signal_a2419e1bd9f44994a1c0f19b524e00b8', 'How do Saviynt runbooks and automation workflows work in real-world scenarios? How do Saviynt runbooks and automation workflows work in real-world scenarios? How do Saviynt runbooks and automation workflows work in real-world scenarios? I’m currently exploring Saviynt Identity Cloud and trying to gain a deeper, practical understanding of how its runbooks, triggers, and automation capabilities work in real-world enterprise environments. From the official documentation, I understand that Saviynt provides strong support for identity lifecycle management (provisioning, deprovisioning, access requests, and governance). However, I’m specifically interested in going beyond the high-level concepts and understanding how these automation features are actually designed, configured, and executed in practice. I would really appreciate insights into the following areas: 🔹 Runbooks & Automation Design How are runbooks defined in Saviynt? Are they configured purely via UI workflows, or is there a way to define them programmatically (JSON/config-based)? What is the underlying execution model (task engine, workflow engine, job orchestration)? How are multi-step automations handled? For example: onboarding → role assignment → approval → provisioning → notification Is there support for conditional branching, retries, and error handling? 🔹 Trigger Mechanisms What types of triggers are supported in real implementations? Event-driven (e.g., user created/updated in HR system) Scheduled jobs (batch-based processing) Approval-based triggers (post-access request approval) API/webhook-driven triggers How does Saviynt handle near real-time vs batch-based execution? 🔹 Integration Capabilities How are integrations typically implemented with systems like: Active Directory / Azure AD (Entra ID) Cloud platforms (AWS, Azure) ITSM tools (ServiceNow, Jira) Are these integrations handled via: Out-of-the-box connectors REST APIs Custom connectors or middleware layers? 🔹 Scripting & Extensibility Is it possible to embed or invoke scripts (e.g., PowerShell, Python) within workflows or runbooks? Can Saviynt call external automation services (e.g., Azure Functions, Logic Apps, Lambda)? How flexible is the platform when implementing custom business logic? 🔹 Enterprise Customization & Best Practices How customizable are workflows for complex enterprise scenarios (RBAC/ABAC, approval chains, exception handling)? What are common architectural patterns used in production deployments? Any best practices for: Scaling automation Managing failures/retries Ensuring auditability and compliance? If anyone has hands-on experience, architectural diagrams, or real-world implementation examples, I’d love to learn from your insights. Thanks in advance! Stack Overflow query: feature request process https://stackoverflow.com/questions/[phone]/how-do-saviynt-runbooks-and-automation-workflows-work-in-real-world-scenarios', 'How do Saviynt runbooks and automation workflows work in real-world scenarios', '[\"how\", \"in\", \"are\", \"automation\", \"saviynt\", \"real\", \"runbooks\", \"workflows\"]', 'positive', 4, 0, NULL, 0, 1, 0.90, 'v1');
INSERT INTO `cleaned_signal` VALUES ('clean_7fc3272f9a904e7cb92a44e3c284bcad', 'signal_ce539b6807384adb82749ea059a6c98c', 'GitHub Actions workflow not scheduling (Schedule trigger not appearing) GitHub Actions workflow not scheduling (Schedule trigger not appearing) GitHub Actions workflow not scheduling (Schedule trigger not appearing) I have developed a Python script and set up a GitHub Actions workflow ( ) to execute it daily at 7 AM KST (22:00 UTC) using a schedule. The issue is that the scheduled automation does not trigger at all. While the (manual trigger) works perfectly fine, there is no indication in the GitHub Actions UI that the workflow is \"scheduled.\" I have already verified the following: No typos in the YAML file. The workflow file is correctly pushed to the default branch ( ). Actions permissions are enabled in the repository settings. The file exists on the default branch. Despite this, the \"clock icon\" or the \"Scheduled\" status is missing from the Actions tab. Has anyone experienced this issue where manual triggers work but the cron schedule is completely ignored by GitHub? I also checked the API ( ) and the state is \'active\', but the \'schedule\' property is missing in the response. Stack Overflow query: manual workflow automation https://stackoverflow.com/questions/[phone]/github-actions-workflow-not-scheduling-schedule-trigger-not-appearing', 'GitHub Actions workflow not scheduling (Schedule trigger not appearing) GitHub Actions workflow not scheduling (Schedul…', '[\"not\", \"actions\", \"is\", \"workflow\", \"github\", \"schedule\", \"trigger\", \"appearing\"]', 'neutral', 2, 0, NULL, 0, 1, 1.00, 'v1');
INSERT INTO `cleaned_signal` VALUES ('clean_80d9bd22d64b44208abefdb317f1a3bd', 'signal_58b82b53ad6a4805a12555afe802ae9a', 'Niri 26.04: Scrollable-tiling Wayland compositor Niri 26.04: Scrollable-tiling Wayland compositor Niri 26.04: Scrollable-tiling Wayland compositor That&#x27;s not actually tiling, is it? To me that reads like fullscreen with workspaces. If one uses a manual tiling window manager like i3 or sway and a large monitor one can divide the screen into separate work areas that each host multiple applications based on their role in one&#x27;s workflow and use less workspaces. Scrolling makes a similar but different workflow practical on small screens where flexibility matters. Hacker News comment query: manual workflow https://github.com/niri-wm/niri/releases/tag/v26.04', 'Niri 26.04: Scrollable-tiling Wayland compositor Niri 26.04: Scrollable-tiling Wayland compositor Niri 26.04: Scrollabl…', '[\"niri\", \"tiling\", \"04\", \"26\", \"compositor\", \"one\", \"scrollable\", \"wayland\"]', 'neutral', 2, 0, NULL, 0, 0, 0.90, 'v1');
INSERT INTO `cleaned_signal` VALUES ('clean_832df15e24b64ea9897eb7cd9e4912b1', 'signal_35ec8d67f8a1493ea3ddfccdb6f82616', 'How to automatically install latest Python package versions in AWS MWAA from S3 requirements.txt without manual updates? How to automatically install latest Python package versions in AWS MWAA from S3 requirements.txt without manual updates? How to automatically install latest Python package versions in AWS MWAA from S3 requirements.txt without manual updates? Body: I am using AWS Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (MWAA) where the file is stored in an S3 bucket and MWAA syncs it during environment updates. Current Setup: is uploaded to S3 MWAA environment installs dependencies from this file Any update to the file in S3 triggers dependency installation My Requirement: I want a fully automated setup where: I upload a to S3 (even once) MWAA always installs the latest available versions of packages automatically No manual intervention is required (no version pinning or editing file repeatedly) For example: Expected Behavior: Whenever MWAA processes the from S3: It should always fetch and install the latest versions of all listed packages from PyPI Concerns / Questions: Does MWAA always install the latest versions when no version is specified, or does it cache previously installed versions? If packages are already installed, will MWAA re-install newer versions automatically on S3 updates? Is there any mechanism in MWAA to force dependency refresh/reinstall from S3 without modifying the file? Are there any AWS-recommended patterns (e.g., dynamic versioning, CI/CD automation, or lifecycle hooks) to achieve this behavior? Goal: To avoid manually updating for every new package version and have a hands-off dependency management system in MWAA, especially for POC or dynamic environments. Any guidance or real-world experience with this would be really helpful. Thanks! Stack Overflow query: manual workflow automation https://stackoverflow.com/questions/[phone]/how-to-automatically-install-latest-python-package-versions-in-aws-mwaa-from-s3', 'How to automatically install latest Python package versions in AWS MWAA from S3 requirements.txt without manual updates', '[\"mwaa\", \"s3\", \"to\", \"versions\", \"in\", \"install\", \"latest\", \"automatically\"]', 'neutral', 2, 0, NULL, 0, 1, 0.90, 'v1');
