INSERT INTO `cleaned_signal` VALUES ('clean_c97d0b7afd694372a15620aa2900bddb', 'signal_a941cdd8116f4d039d089e54cce9fcb3', 'Is maintaining manual per-file and per-function change logs alongside Git considered a professional practice in 2026? Is maintaining manual per-file and per-function change logs alongside Git considered a professional practice in 2026? Is maintaining manual per-file and per-function change logs alongside Git considered a professional practice in 2026? I’m working on a project where we already use modern version control practices: Git with GitFlow branching Pull requests with mandatory reviews Conventional commit messages CI-based checks Despite this, our internal engineering guideline mandates manual change tracking inside source files , in addition to Git history. I’d like feedback from experienced engineers on whether this approach is considered legitimate and professional in 2026 , or if it is generally viewed as redundant in modern workflows. Requirement 1: Mandatory File-Level Modification History Every source file must contain a large comment block at the top that manually tracks changes, including author, date, version, and description. Example (JavaScript) This is required even though Git already tracks the same information via commits and pull requests. Requirement 2: Mandatory Function-Level Change History In addition to file-level history, each function or method must include a similar modification history inside its DocBlock. Example This is enforced during code review. Requirement 3: Strict Formatting Rules That Override Formatters Always use spaces: Inside parentheses: ( $a, $b ) Inside arrays/objects: [ \'id\' => 1 ], { id: 1 } Around operators: $a + $b, a === b After commas The guideline enforces non-standard spacing rules such as: Instead of formatter defaults: Using a formatter like Prettier is allowed, but developers must manually re-adjust formatting afterward to comply with the guideline. Requirement 4: Mandatory DocBlocks for All Functions Every function — including small, internal helpers — must include a full DocBlock describing: Purpose Parameters Return value Change history Even when the function is trivial or self-explanatory. Concern From a developer productivity and maintainability standpoint, this raises several questions: Git already provides authoritative change history — authorship, timestamps, diffs, reviews Manual logs frequently go out of sync Refactoring requires updating many comment blocks Merge conflicts can occur in comment-only sections Formatting rules conflict with automation tools At the same time, the intent is clearly to improve traceability, readability, and auditability. The Actual Question In modern professional software teams (especially in 2025–2026): Is maintaining manual per-file and per-function change logs alongside Git considered a best practice? Do reputable teams still rely on this pattern, or is Git history generally considered sufficient? Are there specific industries (e.g., fintech, regulated environments) where this is still justified? How do modern teams balance documentation requirements with automation and developer productivity? I’m looking for industry-standard perspectives , not company-specific policies. Stack Overflow query: product feedback workflow https://stackoverflow.com/questions/[phone]/is-maintaining-manual-per-file-and-per-function-change-logs-alongside-git-consid', 'Is maintaining manual per-file and per-function change logs alongside Git considered a professional practice in 2026', '[\"is\", \"git\", \"in\", \"per\", \"change\", \"function\", \"file\", \"history\"]', 'neutral', 3, 0, NULL, 0, 1, 0.90, 'v1');
