-based triggers (post-access request approval) API/webhook-driven triggers How does Saviynt handle near real-time vs batch-based execution?\", \"extracted_title\": \"Event-driven (e.g., user created/updated in HR system) Scheduled jobs (batch-based processing) …\", \"relevance_score\": 6.1, \"batch_item_index\": 10, \"extraction_query\": \"feature request automation\", \"extraction_platform\": \"stack_overflow\"}', '2026-04-26 16:57:41');
INSERT INTO `raw_signal` VALUES ('signal_a719438bb8ce4ede8ca33888f9fc60e1', 'batch_8531cdbfce1d41478ca489f679102efa', 'so:79909426', 'https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79909426/automating-repetitive-tasks-with-ruby-and-selenium-webdriver', 'Shruikan', '2026-03-18 00:42:27', 'en', 'Automating repetitive tasks with Ruby and Selenium WebDriver', 'Automating repetitive tasks with Ruby and Selenium WebDriver\n\nAutomating repetitive tasks with Ruby and Selenium WebDriver\n\nI followed the advice from this question and I started to experimenting with Selenium, but I feel like it has to many limitations. Could you recommend something more suitable? A simple program that sends data to a website would be ideal. EDIT// Soooo apparently Selenium will be enough for me. I just need to upgrade it a little. Here is a code I wrote so far ( EDIT2// outdated. I will post updated code below) Suprisingly, it works. But I want it to work better. I would like a window to appear where I could scan serial numbers and type login and password (with \'remember me\' option). Ideally It would look this: EDIT2// When I\'m talking about window, I have something like THAT in mind. Also I\'ve been searching for solutions and I find THIS . I think it will be what I\'m looking for. \"Scan serial number\" means the user has to scan like 4 to 7 hundreds serial numbers per day. It wouldn\'t be bad, BUT as I explained in previous question (link at the begining) it is very time consuming. Here are the steps the user needs to follow: Logging in Select workstation Scan serial number (redirect to next website) Marks him as good or bad (If good fault code = 1, if bad, fault code according to malfunction. That why I need dropdown wit fault codes list) Confirm (redirect to previous website) Go back to step 3 for next serial number As You can see, there is a lot of clicking. What I want to achieve is: Scan multiple serial numbers. Each one goes to separate row, where I can select fault code for this specific serial number (that\'s why I need dropdown with fault codes) Send them all at once and let Selenium do all the clicking. So far I manage to run Beta version, that is working. I run batch file with following code: Here is updated code for Selenium automation (test.rb): Even though it works as intended, I\'m running into some errors. Every instance may cause error, cause sometime the website is slow AF and I get \"NoSuchElementException\" because page didn\'t load in time for element to be present. I know that Selenium has something like but I didn\'t manage to get it to work yet\n\nStack Overflow query: repetitive task automation\n\nhttps://stackoverflow.com/questions/79909426/automating-repetitive-tasks-with-ruby-and-selenium-webdriver', '7fb28e3ce9ea1fdbaf4ba911e9d2ccfdc37fd10e1d5c95f3009df12037e989c3', 'external:so:79909426', 1, 'signal_f9b79a7fa87f4d17a1abe070f4a11e5c', NULL, '{\"tags\": [\"ruby\", \"selenium-webdriver\", \"batch-file\", \"send\"], \"query\": \"repetitive task automation\", \"has_note\": true, \"platform\": \"stack_overflow\", \"batch_label\": \"Stack Overflow auto discovery\", \"has_content\": true, \"import_type\": \"link\", \"import_channel\": \"manual\", \"batch_item_index\": 12}', '2026-04-26 14:22:08');
